  "content": {
    "id": "p41-q8-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "8",
    "prompt": "Solve $\\sin x-\\cos x=0$ for $-\\pi\\le x\\le\\pi$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4},\\ -\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}$.",
      "worked": "Check $\\cos x=0$ first: in the given domain, $x=\\pm\\frac{\\pi}{2}$. At these angles, $\\sin x-\\cos x=\\pm1$, so neither is a solution.\n\nFor $\\cos x\\ne0$, divide by $\\cos x$:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\sin x&=\\cos x\\\\\\tan x&=1.\\end{align*}$$\nThe reference angle is $\\frac{\\pi}{4}$. Tangent is positive in quadrants I and III, so within $-\\pi\\le x\\le\\pi$,\n$$x=\\frac{\\pi}{4},\\ -\\frac{3\\pi}{4}.$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 33
  },
